Q('b1','Count pollen grains','325 microspore mother cells complete normal meiosis. How many microspores result?','1300',['325','650','975'],'Each diploid mother cell gives four haploid microspores: 325 × 4.')
Q('b1','Exine and germ pores','Which substance makes pollen exine highly resistant?','Sporopollenin',['Cellulose alone','Chitin','Pectin alone'],'Sporopollenin forms the resistant exine; it is absent at germ pores.')
Q('b1','Papaya versus potato in hybridisation','During controlled crossing, which flower requires emasculation?','Bisexual potato flower',['Female papaya flower','Both require anther removal','Neither has anthers'],'A female papaya flower lacks anthers; a bisexual potato flower has anthers that could self-pollinate.')
Q('b1','Trace double fertilisation','Triple fusion in a typical angiosperm produces a nucleus that is:','Triploid',['Haploid','Diploid','Tetraploid'],'One haploid male gamete fuses with two haploid polar nuclei, producing the primary endosperm nucleus.')
Q('b2','Label a sperm and explain its parts','The human sperm’s middle piece mainly supplies:','Energy for motility through mitochondria',['Enzymes for egg-covering penetration','The paternal haploid nucleus','The zona pellucida'],'Mitochondria in the middle piece provide energy; the acrosome contains penetration-related enzymes.')
Q('b2','From fertilisation to implantation','Normal human fertilisation generally occurs at the:','Ampullary–isthmic junction of the oviduct',['Cervix','Uterine endometrium','Ovarian cortex'],'The embryo subsequently travels to the uterus, where the blastocyst implants.')
Q('b2','Ovulation to the next menstrual phase','The immediate hormonal trigger for ovulation is the:','LH surge',['Fall in LH to zero','Rise in melatonin','Complete disappearance of FSH throughout the cycle'],'The mid-cycle LH surge causes rupture of the mature Graafian follicle.')
Q('b3','Explain the IVF programme','According to the NCERT distinction, an embryo with more than eight blastomeres is transferred into the:','Uterus (IUT)',['Fallopian tube as ZIFT','Ovary','Cervix for fertilisation'],'ZIFT transfers a zygote or early embryo up to eight blastomeres to the tube; later embryos undergo IUT.')
Q('b3','Is GIFT a form of IVF?','In GIFT, fertilisation occurs:','Inside the body after gamete transfer',['Outside the body before embryo transfer','Inside an artificial uterus','Without gametes'],'GIFT transfers gametes into the fallopian tube; IVF involves fertilisation in the laboratory.')
Q('b3','How hormonal pills act','Which is a textbook action of hormonal contraceptive pills?','Inhibition of ovulation',['Stimulation of repeated ovulation','Surgical blockage of the vas deferens','Destruction of every ovarian follicle'],'The board syllabus describes suppression of ovulation and changes affecting implantation and cervical mucus.')
Q('b4','Identify a heterozygote by test cross','A Tt plant crossed with tt gives the expected dominant:recessive phenotype ratio:','1:1',['3:1','1:2:1','All dominant'],'Tt produces T and t gametes; tt produces only t, yielding Tt and tt equally.')
Q('b4','Birds versus humans','Which parent is heterogametic in birds?','Female (ZW)',['Male (ZZ)','Both parents are XY','Neither parent'],'Female birds produce Z- and W-bearing eggs; males are ZZ.')
Q('b4','Colour-blind father, affected son and daughter','A colour-blind father and normal-vision mother have a colour-blind daughter. The mother must be:','A carrier of the recessive allele',['Homozygous normal','Colour-blind despite the stated normal vision','A source of a Y chromosome to the daughter'],'The daughter needs an affected X from each parent; normal-vision mother is heterozygous.')
Q('b5','Write mRNA from a coding strand','DNA coding strand: 5′–AATGCTAGGCAC–3′. Which mRNA is correct?','5′–AAUGCUAGGCAC–3′',['5′–UUACGAUCCGUG–3′','3′–AAUGCUAGGCAC–5′','5′–AATGCTAGGCAC–3′'],'RNA matches the coding strand’s 5′→3′ sequence with U replacing T.')
Q('b5','Explain four genetic-code properties','Degeneracy of the genetic code means:','Several codons can specify the same amino acid',['One codon normally specifies several amino acids','Every codon is a stop signal','Codons have no fixed length'],'Degeneracy concerns multiple codons for one amino acid; unambiguity means a codon has one usual meaning.')
Q('b5','Explain four genetic-code properties','Which set contains only stop codons?','UAA, UAG, UGA',['AUG, UAA, UGG','AAA, AAG, GAA','UGG, UGC, UGU'],'UAA, UAG and UGA terminate translation in the standard code; AUG is the usual start codon.')
Q('b6','Homologous versus analogous structures','Human arms and whale flippers illustrate:','Homology and divergent evolution',['Analogy and convergent evolution','Homology and convergent evolution','Analogy and divergent evolution'],'They share a basic structural origin while having different functions.')
Q('b6','Industrial melanism','Industrial melanism mainly demonstrates:','Selection acting on existing heritable variation',['Pollution deliberately changing every moth into a dark form','Inheritance of acquired camouflage in one generation','Random mating preventing all evolution'],'Changed backgrounds alter predation and reproductive success of colour variants.')
Q('b6','Derive the equilibrium expression','In Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium, heterozygote frequency is:','2pq',['p²','q²','p + q'],'Random union of gametes gives p² for AA, 2pq for Aa and q² for aa.')
Q('b7','Why a second infection can be prevented','A faster, stronger response on later exposure to the same antigen depends on:','Antigen-specific memory cells',['Only physical skin barriers','The absence of lymphocytes','An entirely new innate receptor formed for each exposure'],'Memory B and T cells enable the secondary adaptive response.')
Q('b7','Ready-made antibodies after snakebite','Administration of preformed antivenom antibodies illustrates:','Artificially acquired passive immunity',['Naturally acquired active immunity','Artificially acquired active immunity','Innate immunity only'],'The recipient receives ready-made antibodies rather than making a primary response to a vaccine.')
Q('b7','Malaria: species and host','The Plasmodium stage introduced into humans by an infected mosquito is the:','Sporozoite',['Gametocyte','Zygote','Ookinete'],'Mosquito salivary glands deliver sporozoites; the initial human multiplication occurs in liver cells.')
Q('b8','Why sewage treatment is biological','During secondary sewage treatment, aerobic microbial flocs cause BOD to:','Decrease',['Increase without limit','Become a measure of dissolved salts only','Remain unchanged while organic matter is consumed'],'Microbes consume organic matter, lowering the oxygen demand of the effluent.')
Q('b8','Milk to curd','Curd formation is promoted by:','Lactic acid bacteria',['Methanogens as the starter','Only bacteriophages','Nitrifying bacteria producing nitric acid'],'LAB such as Lactobacillus produce acids that coagulate and partially digest milk proteins.')
Q('b8','Three sources of biofertilisers','Glomus benefits plants particularly by improving uptake of:','Phosphorus',['Atmospheric oxygen only','Carbon monoxide','Sodium chloride as the sole nutrient'],'Mycorrhizal fungi improve mineral uptake, especially phosphorus, in exchange for carbohydrates.')
Q('b8','Baculoviruses for biological control','A useful feature of nucleopolyhedrovirus biocontrol agents is their:','Narrow host specificity',['Equal toxicity to all vertebrates','Ability to kill every beneficial insect','Replacement of all plant mineral requirements'],'Their target specificity helps reduce harm to non-target organisms.')
Q('b9','EcoRI and recombinant DNA','Which enzyme seals the sugar–phosphate backbone when DNA fragments join?','DNA ligase',['EcoRI','RNA polymerase','Pepsin'],'Restriction enzymes cut DNA; ligase creates the joining phosphodiester bonds.')
Q('b9','Selectable markers and insertional inactivation','In a suitable lacZ blue–white screen, insertional inactivation generally makes recombinant colonies:','White',['Blue','Green','Red'],'Disrupting β-galactosidase prevents the usual blue colour on the chromogenic substrate.')
Q('b9','PCR: heat, primers and Taq','Taq polymerase is useful in PCR because it:','Remains functional through repeated high-temperature cycles',['Separates DNA strands without heat','Creates primers from nothing','Cuts only palindromic DNA'],'Its thermostability supports repeated denaturation, annealing and extension cycles.')
Q('b9','Gel electrophoresis principle','In agarose electrophoresis, DNA normally moves toward the:','Positive electrode; smaller fragments usually move farther',['Negative electrode; smaller fragments usually move farther','Positive electrode; larger fragments always move farther','Negative electrode; larger fragments always move farther'],'DNA is negatively charged; the gel retards larger fragments more strongly.')
Q('b10','How Bt corn resists corn borers','The NCERT cry gene associated with corn-borer resistance is:','cryIAb',['cryIAc only','ADA','lacZ'],'cryIAb (cry1Ab) encodes the Bt toxin used for corn-borer protection in the textbook example.')
Q('b10','RNAi against a tobacco nematode','RNA interference protects tobacco against a nematode by:','Sequence-specific silencing of the target mRNA',['Increasing translation of all nematode genes','Converting every RNA into a protein','Destroying only the plant’s chloroplast DNA'],'Complementary double-stranded RNA triggers silencing of the corresponding message.')
Q('b10','Production of recombinant human insulin','Mature human insulin contains:','A and B chains joined by disulfide bonds',['A, B and retained C-peptide as one unprocessed chain','Only a single C-peptide','Two DNA strands joined by hydrogen bonds'],'Proinsulin’s C-peptide is removed during maturation; mature insulin retains A and B chains.')
Q('b10','Why ADA therapy may need repeated infusions','Modified-lymphocyte therapy for ADA deficiency may need repetition because:','The infused lymphocytes have a limited lifespan',['The ADA gene cannot be expressed in any cell','All lymphocytes are immortal','ADA is a bacterial toxin'],'Correcting a population of finite-lived lymphocytes does not necessarily provide permanent correction.')
Q('b11','J-shaped and S-shaped population growth','Logistic population growth is described by:','dN/dt = rN(K − N)/K',['dN/dt = rN with unlimited resources only','dN/dt = rK²','dN/dt = N/r'],'The logistic term reduces net growth as population N approaches carrying capacity K.')
Q('b11','Meaning of carrying capacity','In a logistic growth model, K represents:','The population the habitat can sustain under the given conditions',['Initial population in every example','The mutation rate','The number of births each second'],'Carrying capacity depends on the habitat’s resources and conditions.')
Q('b11','Mycorrhiza and cattle egret','Cattle egrets feeding on insects disturbed by cattle illustrate:','Commensalism (+/0)',['Mutualism (+/+)','Competition (−/−)','Amensalism (−/0)'],'The egret benefits while the cattle are generally unaffected in this textbook association.')
Q('b12','GPP and NPP','Net primary productivity equals:','GPP − producer respiration',['GPP + producer respiration','Producer respiration − GPP','GPP divided by trophic level'],'NPP is the production remaining after respiratory use by producers.')
Q('b12','Limitations of ecological pyramids','A limitation of simple ecological pyramids is that they:','Do not fully represent food webs and organisms at several trophic levels',['Always include every decomposer','Prove every organism occupies only one trophic level','Show all seasonal changes automatically'],'Pyramids simplify trophic structure and usually omit decomposers.')
Q('b12','An inverted biomass pyramid','An aquatic biomass pyramid may be inverted because phytoplankton have:','Small standing biomass but rapid turnover',['Zero primary productivity','More energy at every higher trophic level','No consumers'],'Rapid production and replacement can support a larger standing biomass of consumers.')
Q('b13','The four major causes of biodiversity loss','The loss of native cichlids after Nile perch introduction illustrates:','Alien-species invasion',['Ex-situ conservation','Artificial pollination','Primary succession'],'An introduced predator can cause major losses among native species.')
Q('b13','Ex-situ conservation','Which is an ex-situ conservation example?','A seed bank',['A national park protecting natural habitat','A biosphere reserve’s natural core','A wildlife sanctuary’s native ecosystem'],'Ex-situ methods conserve organisms or genetic material outside their natural habitat.')
Q('b13','Why tropical regions are species-rich','Which helps explain the general greater species richness of the tropics?','Longer evolutionary time and relatively stable climate',['Repeated glaciation throughout all tropical regions','No available solar energy','The complete absence of ecological specialisation'],'Long evolutionary histories, stability and high energy availability can support diversification.')
